d=50006107"},{"date":"2026-10-21","committee":"Council","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-03","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-10","committee":"Education and Inclusion Scrutiny Committee","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-10","committee":"CJC Joint Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=423&MId=50006093"},{"date":"2026-11-11","committee":"Energy Efficiency and Decarbonisation Cabinet Sub Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-11","committee":"Council","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=163&MId=50005931"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Rhondda Cynon Taff: no banks exercised LOBO options in six months to September, with \u00a331m held","lead":"No banks exercised their option on Rhondda Cynon Taff Council's LOBO loans in the six months to September 2026, according to a report to the council's meeting on 11 November. A LOBO loan is a bank loan where, on set dates, the lender may propose a higher interest rate and the council may then repay the loan in full instead of accepting it.\n\nThe council held \u00a331m of LOBO debt at 30 September, which is 9.7% of its total debt. The report states: \"No banks exercised their option during the six months to September 2026.\"\n\nThe council sets an internal limit on LOBO borrowing of \u00a350m, equal to 20% of its debt portfolio. The table of when its loans fall due shows 17% of debt maturing within 12 months, which the report links to LOBO option dates.","iso":"2026-11-11","paper_title":"Report","paper_url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s63710/63710.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Rhondda Cynon Taff gets clean audit but must fix vehicle disposal records","lead":"Rhondda Cynon Taff Council has received an unqualified audit opinion on its 2025\u201326 accounts and will have them certified on 24 September, six days ahead of the statutory deadline. The Auditor General found the council produced \"a high-quality set of accounts\" and identified no material uncertainties about its ability to continue as a going concern. However, auditors identified a material control weakness: \u00a31.4m of fully depreciated vehicles remain recorded on the asset register despite having been disposed of. The audit report states that \"a material population of fully depreciated vehicles remain recorded in the register,\" indicating the council's vehicle disposal and asset register review processes are \"not operating effectively.\" The council corrected all material misstatements totalling \u00a310.5m before finalisation.","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"Report","paper_url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s60735/60735.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Rhondda Cynon Taff council warns service cuts are 'highly likely' as \u00a359m shortfall looms","lead":"Rhondda Cynon Taff council must find \u00a359.2m over three years from 2027/28 to 2029/30 to balance its budget, the cabinet heard on 21 September. The council warned that revisions to service delivery arrangements and associated staffing levels are \"highly likely\" as a result. This follows three consecutive years of deficits each exceeding \u00a330m and a current-year shortfall of more than \u00a320m. The council's analysis shows that even using reserves \u2014 money a council has set aside \u2014 to cover the shortfall would require over \u00a3150m, and would still leave a \u00a373m permanent reduction in its base budget, signalling that permanent service cuts rather than temporary borrowing must be the main mechanism for addressing the problem.","iso":"2026-09-21","paper_title":"Report","paper_url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s60676/60676.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-11-11","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Report","paper_url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s63710/63710.pdf","headline":"The council's treasury management advisers, Arlingclose, are appointed to 31 March 2027, with an option to extend for up to two further years.","detail":"The paper notes Arlingclose's three-year appointment from 1 April 2024 and an option to extend. It also reports that officers attended webinars and strategy meetings with the advisers during the period.","category":"governance","matters":"The end of the advisory contract within the next six months is a decision point for finance directors and councils looking at treasury advisory arrangements.","figures":"","quote":"for the 3-year period up to 31st March 2027, with an option to extend for up to a further 2 years.","read_on":"2026-11-06","material":true},{"iso":"2026-11-11","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Report","paper_url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s63710/63710.pdf","headline":"General balances earned a 3.72% return against a 3.71% benchmark over six months, with \u00a31.381m of interest received on an average cash balance of \u00a374.026m.","detail":"The paper reports that the council manages its investments in-house, mainly in the Debt Management Account Deposit Facility and other government-backed public sector bodies. The return is measured against the average 7-day SONIA rate, which the paper states is the benchmark for internally managed funds.","category":"income","matters":"A return marginally above the benchmark on a large in-house cash balance is a data point finance officers at other councils can compare against their own treasury returns.","figures":"3.72% return on investments; 3.71% benchmark (SONIA); \u00a31.381m interest received (6 months); \u00a374.026m average cash balance","quote":"General Balances 1.381 74.026 3.72 3.71","read_on":"2026-11-06","material":true},{"iso":"2026-11-11","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Report","paper_url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s63710/63710.pdf","headline":"A 1% change in interest rates would have a one-year revenue impact of \u00a30.666m, based on borrowing and investments at 30 September 2026.","detail":"The paper shows the revenue effect of a 1% rise or fall in interest rates over one year, using the position at 30 September 2026. It is the council's own measure of its exposure to rate movements.","category":"borrowing","matters":"A finance director or lender can use this single sensitivity figure to gauge the council's exposure to rate movements on its debt and cash.","figures":"\u00a30.666m one-year revenue impact of a 1% rise or fall in interest rates","quote":"One year revenue impact of a 1% rise/fall in interest rates 0.666","read_on":"2026-11-06","material":false},{"iso":"2026-11-11","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Report","paper_url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s63710/63710.pdf","headline":"The council's Sustainable Communities for Learning Tranche 1 contribution is modelled at \u00a384.8m, of which \u00a356.1m is borrowing, with revenue implications from 2029/30.","detail":"Tranche 1 of the rolling programme has an estimated total investment of \u00a3280.8m within an approved \u00a3414m programme. The council's modelled contribution is \u00a384.8m, including \u00a328.7m of existing funds and \u00a356.1m of borrowing. The paper says the revenue cost of that borrowing will be phased in from 2029/30, in line with scheme completion.","category":"capital","matters":"Borrowing for the schools programme will add to the council's debt and revenue costs from 2029/30, which is relevant to finance directors planning medium-term budgets and to lenders assessing future borrowing.","figures":"\u00a3414m approved programme (WG in-principle); \u00a3280.8m Tranche 1 total investment; \u00a384.8m council contribution; \u00a328.7m existing funds; \u00a356.1m borrowing; \u00a319.7m current borrowing commitments; 20% borrowing share of total investment","quote":"The latest modelled Council contribution level is \u00a384.8M comprising \u00a328.7M of existing funds and \u00a356.1M of borrowing, as approved by Council on 4th March 2026.","read_on":"2026-11-06","material":true},{"iso":"2026-11-11","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Report","paper_url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s63710/63710.pdf","headline":"Projected 2026/27 total capital spend is \u00a3189.009m, against an original estimate of \u00a3130.808m.","detail":"The capital expenditure indicator shows supported spend projected at \u00a3184.602m against an original \u00a3125.204m, and unsupported spend projected at \u00a34.407m against an original \u00a35.604m. The increase is financed mainly by other capital resources (grants and capital receipts), projected at \u00a3177.666m against an original \u00a3118.268m.","category":"capital","matters":"A large rise in projected capital spend financed by grants and receipts shifts the council's funding mix, which finance directors and lenders watch when assessing capital delivery and external funding dependence.","figures":"\u00a3189.009m projected 2026/27 total spend (original \u00a3130.808m); \u00a3184.602m supported spend (original \u00a3125.204m); \u00a3177.666m other capital resources (original \u00a3118.268m); \u00a311.343m borrowing (original \u00a312.540m)","quote":"Total spend 130.808 189.009 96.652 91.326","read_on":"2026-11-06","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-11-11"},"name":"Rhondda Cynon Taff","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-11-14","since_iso":"2026-10-15","distress":false,"headline":"The council's treasury management advisers, Arlingclose, are appointed to 31 March 2027, with an option to extend for up to two further years.","lead":"The end of the advisory contract within the next six months is a decision point for finance directors and councils looking at treasury advisory arrangements.","also":["20 other findings in this window","44 papers caught"],"watch":"Overview and Scrutiny 2022-2027 Committee on 2026-11-16","n_findings":21,"n_documents":44,"top_paper_title":"Report","top_paper_url":"https://rctcbc.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s63710/63710.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":69,"news_drafts_total":3}}